 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 3,
   "text": "ABERTAWE / SWANSEA. Croeso! (Welcome!) North: Bannau Brycheiniog, the Brecon Beacons. West: G\u0175yr, the Gower."
  },
  {
   "x": 1,
   "y": 39,
   "text": "G\u0174YR / GOWER. Britain's first Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty. Worm's Head, Rhossili and the cockle beds of Penclawdd."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 3,
   "plaque": "swansea_name",
   "title": "Abertawe",
   "text": "Abertawe means 'mouth of the Tawe'. The English name is probably Norse, Sveinsey, Sveinn's island or inlet, from the Viking traders who sheltered here. Two names, one town, both used every day."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "swansea_liberty",
   "title": "The Swans",
   "text": "Swansea City play at the stadium opened in 2005, which the Ospreys rugby side shared for years. In 2011 the Swans became the first Welsh club in the Premier League, and in 2013 they won the League Cup at Wembley, 5-0."
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "swansea_copperopolis",
   "title": "Copperopolis",
   "text": "In the nineteenth century the lower Swansea Valley smelted much of the world's copper, with ore shipped in from Cornwall and later from Chile and Cuba. The money built the town; the smoke killed every plant for miles. The Hafod-Morfa works closed in 1980, and the valley has been greened again since."
  },
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 9,
   "plaque": "swansea_cwmdonkin",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Cwmdonkin Park",
   "text": "Dylan Thomas played here as a boy, and the park turns up in his poem 'The Hunchback in the Park'. A memorial stone near the old reservoir carries lines from his poem 'Fern Hill'."
  },
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 16,
   "plaque": "swansea_birthplace",
   "title": "5 Cwmdonkin Drive",
   "text": "Dylan Thomas was born in this house on 27 October 1914, and wrote a great many of his early poems in the box bedroom upstairs. His father taught English at the grammar school down the road."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 30,
   "plaque": "swansea_market",
   "title": "Swansea Market",
   "text": "The biggest indoor market in Wales. The old Victorian market burned out in the Three Nights' Blitz of February 1941, and the new one opened under its great glass roof in 1961. Cockles, laverbread and Welsh cakes hot off the bakestone are the order of the day."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 29,
   "local": {"cy": "Castell Abertawe"},
   "plaque": "swansea_castle",
   "title": "Swansea Castle",
   "text": "A Norman castle founded around 1106, now hemmed in by the shops and offices of the centre. The arcaded parapet on the surviving range is the castle's best-kept secret, and it isn't very well kept."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "swansea_blitz",
   "title": "Three Nights' Blitz",
   "text": "On 19, 20 and 21 February 1941 German bombers wrecked the heart of Swansea, a port they wanted to close. Over two hundred people were killed. Dylan Thomas walked the ruins afterwards and said, simply, that our Swansea was dead."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 37,
   "plaque": "swansea_waterfront",
   "title": "National Waterfront Museum",
   "text": "Part of the national museum of Wales, built in 2005 around a red-brick 1901 dock warehouse. It tells the story of industry and innovation in Wales: copper, coal, tinplate, ships and the people who worked them."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 43,
   "plaque": "swansea_dylan",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Dylan Thomas",
   "text": "Swansea's most famous son, born 1914, died in New York in 1953 aged 39. He called it an 'ugly, lovely town'. His play for voices, Under Milk Wood, was first broadcast on the BBC in 1954 with Richard Burton reading."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 46,
   "plaque": "swansea_jack",
   "title": "Swansea Jack",
   "text": "Swansea Jack was a black retriever who lived by the docks in the 1930s and pulled a reported 27 people out of the water. He has a monument on the prom, and some say it's why Swansea folk are called Jacks."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 46,
   "plaque": "swansea_railway",
   "title": "The Mumbles Railway",
   "text": "On 25 March 1807 the Oystermouth Railway began carrying fare-paying passengers in a horse-drawn carriage, the first passenger railway service in the world. It ran on horse, sail, steam and electricity, and closed in January 1960. The prom path follows its line."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 47,
   "local": {"cy": "Bae Abertawe"},
   "plaque": "swansea_tide",
   "title": "Swansea Bay",
   "text": "The Bristol Channel has one of the biggest tidal ranges in the world, and at a big spring tide the water here rises and falls around ten metres. Low tide uncovers a mile of sand; high tide takes it back, quickly."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 41,
   "local": {"cy": "Y Mwmbwls"},
   "plaque": "swansea_mumbles",
   "title": "The Mumbles",
   "text": "Named, the story goes, by French sailors after 'mamelles', breasts, for the two rounded islands off the head. The sailors had been at sea a long time. The lighthouse on the outer island has shown a light since 1794."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 38,
   "local": {"cy": "Castell Ystumllwynarth"},
   "plaque": "swansea_oystermouth",
   "title": "Oystermouth Castle",
   "text": "Founded by the Norman William de Londres in the early 1100s, rebuilt in stone by the de Breose lords of Gower in the 1200s, and wonderfully preserved. Mumbles grew rich on oysters dredged from the bay, and the village name Oystermouth remembers it."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 45,
   "plaque": "swansea_lifeboat",
   "title": "The Mumbles Lifeboat",
   "text": "On 23 April 1947 the Mumbles lifeboat went out to the Samtampa, aground in a storm off Sker Point. All eight lifeboat crew were lost, with every soul aboard the ship. The station launched again within days, as it still does."
  },
  {
   "x": 69,
   "y": 26,
   "local": {"cy": "Mynydd Cilfái"},
   "plaque": "swansea_kilvey",
   "title": "Kilvey Hill",
   "text": "The hill east of the Tawe stood bare for a century under the copper smoke. Tree planting from the 1960s brought it back; the bracken and pines up here are the valley's comeback."
  },
  {
   "x": 60,
   "y": 39,
   "text": "SWANSEA MARINA. Built in the 1980s in the old South Dock. The boats are newer. The gulls are not."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 45,
   "plaque": "swansea_laugharne",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Boathouse, Laugharne",
   "text": "In 1949 Dylan Thomas moved with his family into the Boathouse at Laugharne, a little town on the T\u00e2f estuary west of here, and wrote in a shed above the water with a view of the herons. Under Milk Wood, his play for voices, grew out of the town. He died in New York on 9 November 1953, aged 39, and is buried at St Martin's in Laugharne under a plain white cross. The BBC broadcast Under Milk Wood in January 1954 with Richard Burton as First Voice. The shed is still there, and the view has not changed its mind."
  }
 ],
